Replace every transposition by an odd product of adjacent transpositions. For a permutation $sigma$, call ${i,j}$ an inversion when $i<j$ but $sigma(i)>sigma(j)$. Multiplication by an adjacent transposition changes the number of inversions by exactly $1$, so the number of inversions of a product of $k$ adjacent transpositions has the same parity as $k$. The identity has no inversions, hence $k$ is even.
